  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure and upper level ridging keeps the region\ndry with weak boundary layer flow. This will promote valley fog\nonce again overnight tonight into Wednesday morning. Some areas\nmay see dense fog which could impact certain areas with patchy \ndense fog through the morning which would lower visibility and\npossibly impact driving conditions.\n\nTemperatures will continue to warm through the work week \ngetting anomalously above the average for this time of year by\nThursday. However, by Friday temperatures will start to come \ndown in values due to cloud coverage and a cold frontal boundary\naffecting the region possibly into or through the weekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA cold front is forecast to affect the region on Friday \nafternoon pushing into the area from the north which will \nsubsequently spread rain and chances for thunderstorms \nthroughout the day toward the south. The lack of any modest \nshear and high instability will limit the severe weather threat,\nhowever a few stronger storms cannot be ruled out. Heavy \ndownpours are possible with enough DCAPE and PWAT values to \nsupport this solution. Very localized instances of flash \nflooding could be possible along low lying and flood prone areas\nwho are hit repeatedly for Friday and even on Saturday. WPC has\nus in a marginal threat for excessive rainfall for much of the \narea on Saturday. Depending on how long the frontal boundary \nmeanders across the area will determine the severity threat of \nany real hydro issues. \n\nMedium range models have the front slowing down and possibly \nbecoming stationary as high pressure from the south does not \nallow for safe and quick frontal passage. This means that the \narea may have to endure unsettled weather for much of the \nweekend until stronger high pressure builds in from the \nnorthwest and a clipper type system finally brushes the quasi- \nstationary frontal boundary out of the region pushing it well to\nour south by Monday morning according to models. This model \nsolution means that more active weather will likely stick around\nthrough the weekend and possibly give the area some issues with\nhydro on Saturday albeit localized and isolated since the area \nhas been dry but will likely be slightly primed due to any \nconvection that occurs on Friday.\n\nIf the frontal boundary has trouble exiting on Sunday any \nprimed areas from previous rainfall could promote susceptible \nareas to flash flooding under heavy thunderstorm or downpour \nactivity throughout Sunday and into Monday or when the front\nfinally exits far enough to the south to mitigate impacts.\n\nStronger high pressure will filter in Monday and get the region\nback into a settled weather pattern with temperatures returning  \nback down to around the climatological average for this time of \nyear.",
